<edge source="europe_political_shift::far_right_support" target="global_bond_repricing::portfolio_reallocation" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.65</data>
  <data key="d36">A rapid increase in far‑right electoral support in one or more EU states triggers cross‑border risk‑off flows and reweighting of exposures (out of peripheral sovereigns/equities into core sovereigns, FX and safe assets), accelerating portfolio reallocation.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">europe_political_shift</data>
  <data key="d39">global_bond_repricing</data>
</edge>
<edge source="europe_political_shift::far_right_support" target="gulf_maritime_conflict::conflict_intensity" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.55</data>
  <data key="d36">Electoral gains for far‑right parties in Europe shift government foreign‑policy priorities toward transactional ties and reduced support for multilateral deterrence (weaker sanctions/coordinated maritime escorts), which lowers the cost for Gulf actors and proxies to escalate strikes and maritime attacks.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">europe_political_shift</data>
  <data key="d39">gulf_maritime_conflict</data>
</edge>
<edge source="europe_political_shift::far_right_support" target="russia_ukraine_war::arms_transfer_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Rising electoral support for far‑right parties incentivizes coalition partners or governing parties to adopt more isolationist or Russia‑sympathetic stances and to oppose or curtail lethal military aid, lowering arms deliveries to Ukraine.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">europe_political_shift</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="europe_political_shift::far_right_support" target="us_china_tech_trade::export_controls_tightnes" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Electoral gains by radical-right parties push several European governments to adopt security‑first trade stances and blacklist or restrict Chinese access to sensitive tech, prompting the US to harmonize and broaden its own export restrictions to avoid regulatory gaps and transatlantic circumvention.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">europe_political_shift</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_trade</data>
</edge>
<edge source="europe_political_shift::far_right_support" target="us_china_tech_trade::semiconductor_prod_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">When European politics tilt toward restrictive, nationalist policies and tighter FDI screening, Chinese firms and state-backed programs accelerate domestic semiconductor capacity buildout to substitute for blocked access to European fabs and design assets.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">europe_political_shift</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_trade</data>
</edge>
